{
  "text": "A database index is a data structure that helps a database to find records more efficiently. Without an index, the database may need to examine every row in a table to find matching data. This operation is commonly called a full table scan. Imagine a users table containing ten million records. If an application frequently searches for a user using an email address, scanning all ten million rows for every request would be inefficient. Creating an index on the email column gives the database a faster way to locate the required record. A common database index uses a B-tree structure. Instead of reading rows sequentially, the database can navigate through organized index entries to quickly identify the location of matching records. This can dramatically reduce the amount of data that must be inspected. Indexes are particularly useful for columns frequently used in WHERE conditions, JOIN conditions, sorting, and sometimes grouping. Primary keys are usually indexed automatically by the database. However, indexes are not free. Every index consumes storage space. More importantly, indexes must be updated whenever related data is inserted, updated, or deleted. A table with too many indexes may therefore have fast reads but slower writes. Developers should avoid creating indexes blindly on every column. The usefulness of an index depends on how the application queries the data. A column with very few distinct values may not always benefit greatly from a simple index. For example, consider a status column containing only ACTIVE and INACTIVE. If almost all rows are ACTIVE, an index on that column alone may not significantly reduce the amount of data the database must read. Composite indexes contain more than one column. Suppose an application frequently searches orders using customer_id and created_date together. A composite index may improve those queries. However, column order inside the index matters because databases can use composite indexes differently depending on the query. Database query planners decide whether to use an index. Even when an index exists, the database may choose a table scan if it estimates that scanning the table is cheaper. Developers can inspect query execution plans using tools such as EXPLAIN. Execution plans show whether indexes are being used and help identify expensive operations. Indexes should therefore be designed using real query patterns and performance measurements rather than assumptions.",
  "maxLength": 750
}


